#metadata(title = "Mote Mixed-Size Inline Runs", author = "Mote")

#document(
  page = { size = "letter" },
  margin = { top = pt(54), right = pt(72), bottom = pt(54), left = pt(72) }
)

#style(
  name = "Normal",
  type = "paragraph",
  run = { size = pt(11), color = "#111827" },
  paragraph = {
    spacing = {
      after = pt(8),
      line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 }
    }
  }
)

# Mixed-Size Inline Runs

PDF and PNG should match Word: runs on the same line share a baseline, and only a line containing larger text grows taller.

## Forced Line Boundaries

#paragraph(style = "Normal", spacing = { after = pt(8), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } })[
Eleven-point line before.#line_break()
Eleven-point lead, #span(size = pt(24), bold = true, color = "#1d4ed8")[twenty-four point inline], eleven-point tail.#line_break()
Eleven-point line after with no overlap.
]

## Natural Wrapping

#paragraph(style = "Normal", spacing = { after = pt(8), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } })[
This paragraph wraps normally before and after a #span(size = pt(24), bold = true, color = "#047857")[large inline segment that establishes its line height] while the surrounding wrapped lines remain at the ordinary eleven-point height.
]

The final paragraph must begin below the wrapped paragraph without collision.

## Pagination Flow

The remaining probes deliberately continue onto a second page without a forced page break. A mixed-size line may move as a unit when its measured height does not fit the remaining frame.

#paragraph(style = "Normal", spacing = { after = pt(8), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } })[
Flow probe one begins at eleven points, introduces #span(size = pt(22), bold = true, color = "#b45309")[a twenty-two point inline run that controls only its own line], and returns to ordinary text for the following wrapped line.
]

#paragraph(style = "Normal", spacing = { after = pt(8), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } })[
Flow probe two aligns small text with #span(size = pt(26), bold = true, color = "#be123c")[a twenty-six point segment wrapping across two lines] before returning to an ordinary eleven-point tail.
]

## Boundary Table Cell

#table(width = inch(6.5), layout = "fixed", alignment = "center")[
  #row[
    #cell(
      width = inch(6.5),
      fill = "#f8fafc",
      margin = { all = pt(6) },
      borders = { all = { style = "single", size = 6, color = "#94a3b8" } }
    )[
      #paragraph(style = "Normal", spacing = { after = pt(0), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } })[
Eleven-point line before.#line_break()
Eleven-point lead, #span(size = pt(24), bold = true, color = "#7c3aed")[twenty-four point inline], eleven-point tail.#line_break()
Eleven-point line after with no border collision.
      ]
    ]
  ]
]

#paragraph(style = "Normal", spacing = { after = pt(24), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } })[
Boundary check: the complete table stays on page one.
]

#paragraph(style = "Normal", spacing = { after = pt(8), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } })[
Flow probe three provides a compact lead, #span(size = pt(20), bold = true, color = "#0369a1")[twenty-point text in the middle], and enough trailing text to prove that the next wrapped line returns to the normal line height.
]

#paragraph(style = "Normal", spacing = { after = pt(8), line = { rule = "multiple", value = 1.15 } })[
Flow probe four places #span(size = pt(28), bold = true, color = "#6d28d9")[the tallest twenty-eight point inline run near the page boundary] so PDF and PNG must reserve the complete measured line before continuing.
]

## List Flow

- Eleven-point bullet lead with #span(size = pt(22), bold = true, color = "#0f766e")[twenty-two point item text on the same baseline] and an ordinary wrapped tail below it.
- A second bullet begins normally, reaches #span(size = pt(24), bold = true, color = "#1d4ed8")[a twenty-four point inline checkpoint], then finishes without colliding with the next marker.

1. Ordered marker alignment is checked beside #span(size = pt(22), bold = true, color = "#b45309")[a twenty-two point first line] and its ordinary continuation.
2. The final item contains #span(size = pt(24), bold = true, color = "#7c3aed")[one last twenty-four point run] before the closing paragraph.

The closing eleven-point paragraph must remain below every mixed-size list item, with no overlap at the bottom of the second page.
